    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my security+ exam and i'm a bit confused. i get that hashing turns a password into a string of characters, but why do we actually need to add a 'salt' to it? like if the hash is already unique, why does the extra step matter?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, cryptography concepts can be counterintuitive at first. Adding a salt to a hash actually helps prevent hash collisions, where two different passwords would produce the same hash value. This occurs because the salt is mixed with the password before hashing, so even if two users choose the same password, their hashes will be different because of the varying salts.",
