    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get the part about the hashes being different for the same password. but i was reading about something called \"rainbow tables\" in my textbook. how does salting specifically stop someone from using those tables to crack the passwords?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashes are calculated independently of each other even if the input is the same, so even if a crack has been precomputed, the use of a salt means the attacker must start from scratch for each user, rendering the rainbow table useless.",
